-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
This paper is directed towards extending the theory of the condition
number to sparse systems of polynomial equations.  In general terms,
this is potentially of great interest, since in practice most systems
of interest are sparse.  While I cannot say whether the long run goal,
namely a theory of the complexity of sparse homotopy methods, is
likely to be attained, it certainly strikes me as a very useful
contribution.

At any rate the current version has various defects of authorship.
(As someone afflicted with ``proof reader's blindness,'' I have great
sympathy.)  It seems clear that the next step is to fix these.  The
ones that I found (which give some indication of how far I was able to
read) are detailed below.

\def\booboo#1{\medskip \itemitem{($\bullet$)} #1}

\booboo{
At the point where Theorem 2 is stated, the way that $(A_1, C_1,
\ldots, A_n, C_n)$ determine a random polynomial system has not been
described.  Similarly, in Definition 3 the status of $C$ is unclear.}

\booboo{
In Example 3, it appears that $C$ should be an $M \times M$ matrix.}

\booboo{
Example 3 refers to ``the above lemma,'' but there is no earlier
lemma.}

\booboo{
At the end of Example 3 there is appears to be a typo in the first
element of the triple.}

\booboo{
In the statement of Lemma 1, it seems that the conclusion holds for
all $p \in {\bf C}^n$.  Also, Lemma 1 refers to $v_A$, but this
function is not defined until the next page.  In addition, the
notation $P_{v_A(p)}$ is undefined.}

\booboo{
Though not a logical flaw, it would clearly be an improvement to place 
the definition of ${\hat V}_A$ in Example 3 and the assertion of Lemma 
2 in displayed equations.  The latter should be concluded with a period.}

